<title>The decay of lead in the bloodstream</title>
<variantControl numVariants="1" />

<number name="stage" hide="true">1</number>


<section><title>Introduction</title>

<p>As 
<ref to="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead">lead</ref>
 is a strong poison, small amounts of lead in the body can cause 
<ref to="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead_poisoning">lead poisoning</ref>, leading to serious health problems.  The body does eliminate lead, such as through urine, but the elimination is slow.</p>

  <aside>
    <title>How slow?</title>

    <p>The elimination of lead from the bones can take decades. But, since  that sounds too depressing, we'll consider the lead level in the bloodstream, which is much faster. If no further sources of lead are introduced, then lead will be slowly eliminated from the bloodstream in a matter of weeks.  For our model, below, we'll imagine that the amount of lead decreases by 11% each week.</p>
    
  </aside>

<p>Although the lead level changes continuously over time, let's just model weekly snapshots of the lead concentration.  (You could imagine that a doctor is testing a patient's lead concentrations each week, and we'll work with just these weekly numbers.) We'll use a 
<ref to="doenet:7C1w6bKbkKghbh9wUtwzxF">discrete dynamical system</ref>, where the time interval is one week. We'll start at <m>t=0</m>.  The value <m>t=1</m> will represent one week later, <m>t=2</m> will represent two weeks later, etc.</p>


<p>A bit more notation: we'll write the lead level at time <m>t</m> as <m>c_t</m>. So <m>c_3</m> is the lead level after three weeks.</p>

  <aside>
    <title>Why use subscripts?</title>

    <p>For some reason, using a subscript for time <m>t</m>, as in <m>c_t</m>, is a common convention when time is discrete steps. Think of the concentrations as a sequence of number <m>c_0</m>, <m>c_1</m>, <m>c_2</m>, etc.</p>
    
  </aside>


  <p>We're not going to focus much on units, but in this example, let's say concentration is measured in μg/dl (micrograms per deciliter). For simplicity, write the values of <m>c_t</m> without units, so the equation <m>c_3=91</m> means the lead concentration was 91 μg/dl in week three.</p>
  

<p>Quick check-in. How would you use this notation to express the fact the lead concentration was 73 μg/dl in week 11?
  <answer name="c1173">
    <award><math>c_11 = 73</math></award>
  </answer>
</p>
  
<hint>
  To enter a subscript for <m>c_{11}</m>, either type <c>_</c> or use the math keyboard. (Click the keyboard button in the lower left corner to open the math keyboard.) In the keyboard, click the <c>$%∞</c> tab and then click the <m>a_b</m> button to get a subscript.
</hint>

  <hint hide="$c1173.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $c1173.creditAchieved = 1">
  The equation <m>c_{11} = 73</m> is the statement that the lead concentration in week 11 was 73 μg/dl.
</hint>
<p>What's the equation that states the lead concentration was 31 μg/dl in day 21?
  <answer name="c2131">
    <award><math>c_3 = 31</math></award>
    <award credit="0" name="notDays"><math>c_21 = 31</math></award>
  </answer>
</p>
<hint hide="$c2131.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $c2131.creditAchieved = 1">
  The equation <m>c_{3} = 31</m> is the statement that the lead concentration in day 21 (i.e., week 3) was 31 μg/dl.
</hint>
<feedback condition="$notDays">
  Gotcha!  Remember that time <m>t</m> is measured in weeks, not days.  The quantity <m>c_{21}</m> is the lead concentation in week 21, which is day <m>21 \times 7 = <math simplify="true">27*7</math></m>.  Day 21 is which week?
</feedback>

<p>Let's add dynamics by assuming that the lead level in the blood decreases by 11% each week. That means, after one week, what percentage of the lead is remaining in the bloodstream?  
  <answer name="eightnine">
    <award>89</award>
    <award>89%</award>
  </answer>
</p>
<hint hide="$eightnine.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $eightnine.creditAchieved = 1">
 The percentage left is <m>100-11=89</m>.
</hint>

<p>First, let pretend we start with an initial lead concentration of 50 μg/dl, i.e., <m>c_0=50</m>. Then, <m>c_1 = </m> <answer name="c50w1">50*0.89</answer>, i.e., after one week the lead concentration is <math simplify="true">$c50w1</math> μg/dl.  If we wait one more week, the lead concentration should drop to <m>c_2 =</m> <answer name="c50w2">50*0.89*0.89</answer>, i.e., in week two, we're down to a concentration of <math simplify="true">$c50w2</math> μg/dl.</p>

<p>Let's make this a little harder.  Let's say we don't know the initial lead concentration so we just represent it by the parameter <m>c_0</m>.  Now, your answer for the later concentrations will depend on <m>c_0</m>.  Given that the lead concentration decreases by 11% each week, what's the formula for the lead concentration after one week? <m>c_1=</m>
<answer name="c1generic">0.89 c_0</answer>.</p>

<matchesPattern name="c1g_has_c0" hide="true" pattern="()c_0">$c1generic</matchesPattern>

  <feedback condition="not $c1g_has_c0 and $c1generic.responseHasBeenSubmitted and $c1generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
    The value of <m>c_1</m> must be <m>c_0</m> multiplied by something.
  </feedback>
    <feedback condition="$c1g_has_c0 and $c1generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
    Good start. Your answer does multiply <m>c_0</m> by something. In one week, you should multiply <m>c_0</m> by what value? <answer weight="0">0.89</answer>
  </feedback>
  
<hint hide="$c1generic.numSubmissions &lt; 3 
 or $c1generic.numSubmissions > 4 or $c1generic.creditAchieved = 1">
  Just multiply <m>c_0</m> by 0.89.
</hint>
<hint hide="$c1generic.numSubmissions &lt; 5 or $c1generic.creditAchieved = 1">
 <m>c_1 = 0.89 c_0</m> 
</hint>

<p>The lead, of course, doesn't know what week it is.  We assume its concentration always decays by 11% over the course the week.  Given that <m>c_8</m> is the concentration in week 8, what's the formula for the concentration in week 9?
<m>c_9=</m>
<answer name="c9generic">0.89 c_8</answer>.</p>

  <matchesPattern name="c9g_has_c8" hide="true" pattern="()c_8">$c9generic</matchesPattern>

  <feedback condition="not $c9g_has_c8 and $c9generic.responseHasBeenSubmitted and $c9generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
    The value of <m>c_9</m> must be <m>c_8</m> multiplied by something.
  </feedback>
    <feedback condition="$c9g_has_c8 and $c9generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
    Good start. Your answer does multiply <m>c_8</m> by something. In one week, you should multiply <m>c_8</m> by what value? <answer weight="0">0.89</answer>
  </feedback>
<hint hide="$c9generic.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $c9generic.numSubmissions > 4 or $c9generic.creditAchieved = 1">
  Just multiply <m>c_8</m> by 0.89.
</hint>
<hint hide="$c9generic.numSubmissions &lt; 5 or $c9generic.creditAchieved = 1">
 <m>c_9 = 0.89 c_8</m> 
</hint>

<p>You could probably now write the formula for <m>c_{33}</m> in terms of <m>c_{32}</m>, but can you take it to the next level?  Let's not even give a number for the week but leave it generic in terms of the variable <m>t</m>.  Since <m>c_t</m> is the lead concentration in week <m>t</m>, what's the formula for the concentration the following week, which is week <m>t+1</m>? </p>
<p><m>c_{t+1}=</m>
<answer name="ctp1generic">0.89 c_t</answer>.</p>

    <matchesPattern name="ctp1g_has_ct" hide="true" pattern="()c_t">$ctp1generic</matchesPattern>

  <feedback condition="not $ctp1g_has_ct and $ctp1generic.responseHasBeenSubmitted and $ctp1generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
    The value of <m>c_{t+1}</m> must be <m>c_t</m> multiplied by something.
  </feedback>
    <feedback condition="$ctp1g_has_ct and $ctp1generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
    Good start. Your answer does multiply <m>c_t</m> by something. In one week, you should multiply <m>c_t</m> by what value? <answer weight="0">0.89</answer>
  </feedback>

  
<hint hide="$ctp1generic.numSubmissions &lt; 3 or $ctp1generic.numSubmissions > 4 or $ctp1generic.creditAchieved = 1">
  Just multiply <m>c_t</m> by 0.89.
</hint>
<hint hide="$ctp1generic.numSubmissions &lt; 5 or $ctp1generic.creditAchieved = 1">
 <m>c_{t+1} = 0.89 c_t</m> 
</hint>

<p hide="$ctp1generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1">
  That's great!  You have now derived the dynamical rule for the discrete dynamical system describing lead decay.  Now we can look at the behavior of this model.
</p>

<updateValue disabled="$ctp1generic.creditAchieved &lt; 1" target="$stage" newValue="2" hide="$stage >=2">
  <label>continue</label>
</updateValue>
</section>


<section hide="$stage &lt; 2"><title>Exploring the lead decay model</title>

<p>The discrete dynamical 
<me>c_{t+1} = 0.89 c_t</me>
models the decay of lead in the bloodstream.  We're going to simulate this model to explore its behavior.  You could probably use your calculator to quickly multiply by 0.89 many times, but we also have a <ref to="doenet:7CkDpewb8Wp9Mng4XjxDHt">function iteration applet</ref> applet that can do the work for us.
</p>

<p>To use the function iteration applet, you have to specify what function you are applying for each time step.  In this case, we are multiplying by 0.89 at each time step, so we are repeated applying the function <m>f(c) = 0.89c</m>.</p>

<p>Change the definition of <m>f(c)</m> in the below box, enter your desired initial condition (or drag the <copy prop="styleDefinition" source="x0" /> point on the vertical axis), and you're good to go.  Simply click the iterate button to simulate the dynamical systems and calculate values of the lead concentration.</p>
  
<setup>
  <number name="nIterations">0</number>
  <function variables="$varName" name="f">$fformula</function>
  <number name="zeroFixed" fixed="true">0</number>
  <functionIterates initialValue="$initialValue" function="$f" numIterates="$nIterations" name="fIterates" />
  <mathList extend="$fIterates.iterates" name="allIterates" displayDigits="4" />
  <repeat for="$allIterates" name="iterateVertices" valueName="v" indexName="i"><point draggable="false" styleNumber="2">($i, $v)</point></repeat>
  <math extend="$fIterates.finalIterate" name="finalValue" displayDigits="4" />
  
</setup>


<sideBySide>
  <stack>
    
    <p><m>f($varName) =</m> <mathInput name="fformula" prefill="c+1" /> <m>{$varName}_0 = </m> <mathInput name="initialValue" prefill="5" displayDigits="4" /></p>

    <updateValue target="$nIterations" newValue="$nIterations+1">
      <label>iterate</label>
    </updateValue>
    <updateValue target="$nIterations" newValue="0">
      <label>reset</label>
    </updateValue>

    <text name="text_varName" hide="true">$varName</text>
    <graph xmin="-0.1$tmax" xmax="$tmax" ymin="$xmin" ymax="$xmax">
      <xLabel>n</xLabel>
      <yLabel>$text_varName</yLabel>
      
      <lineSegment endpoints="(0,$xmin) (0, $xmax)" fixed="true" name="stateLine" />
    
      <point name="x0" labelPosition="left">($zeroFixed, $initialValue)
        <label><m>{$varName}_0</m></label>
      </point>
      <polyline draggable="false" vertices="(0, $initialValue) $iterateVertices" styleNumber="2" />
      $iterateVertices
      <point draggable="false" styleNumber="3" name="xn" hide="$nIterations = 0" labelPosition="top">($zeroFixed, $finalValue)
        <label><m>{$varName}_{$nIterations} = $finalValue</m></label></point>
      <lineSegment endpoints="$xn ($nIterations, $finalValue)" hide="$nIterations=0" styleNumber="3" draggable="false" name="linkLine" />
    </graph>
  </stack>
<stack>
  <tabular>
    <row header="true"><cell>Iterate values</cell></row>
    <row><cell><m>{$varName}_0 = $initialValue</m></cell></row>
    <repeat for="$allIterates" valueName="v" indexName="i"><row><cell><m>{$varName}_{$i} = $v</m></cell></row></repeat>
  </tabular>
</stack>
</sideBySide>

<aside><title>Additional controls</title>

<p>Variable name: <mathInput name="varName" prefill="c" /></p>
<p><m>{$varName}_{min}</m>: <mathInput name="xmin" prefill="-10" /></p>
<p><m>{$varName}_{max}</m>: <mathInput name="xmax" prefill="80" /></p>
<p><m>t_{max}</m>: <mathInput name="tmax" prefill="20" /></p>

</aside> 



<p>Since the applet is doing all the calculations, you can concentrate on the big picture of the elimination of lead from the bloodstream.  In particular, we're interested in how fast the lead is eliminated as predicted from the model.</p>

<p>If you start with an initial condition of <m>c_0=64</m> μg/dl, how many weeks does it take to get down to a concentration of 32 μg/dl?  
<answer name="c64To32">
<award><ceil>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</math></award>
</answer>
To 16 μg/dl? 
<answer name="c64To16">
<award><ceil>log(1/4)/log(0.89)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/4)/log(0.89)</math></award>
</answer> 
To 8 μg/dl? 
<answer name="c64To8">
<award><ceil>log(1/8)/log(0.89)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/8)/log(0.89)</math></award>
</answer> 
</p>

$c64To32.feedbacks
$c64To16.feedbacks
$c64To8.feedbacks

<p>If instead you started with a larger concentration, 100 μg/dl, how long (in weeks) does it take to drop to 50 μg/dl? 
  <answer name="c100To50">
<award><ceil>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</math></award>
</answer>
</p>
  
$c100To50.feedbacks

<p>If instead you started with a smaller concentration, 10 μg/dl), how many weeks does it take for the contentration to drop by half?  <answer name="c10To5">
<award><ceil>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</math></award>
</answer>
</p>
  
$c10To5.feedbacks



<p>If the body eliminates 11% of the lead from the bloodstream every week, how many weeks does it take the body to eliminate half of the lead?
<answer name="c11half">
<award><ceil>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/2)/log(0.89)</math></award>
</answer></p>

$c11half.feedbacks

<p>If instead the body eliminated only 4% of the lead from the bloodstream each week, how many weeks would it take to eliminate half of the lead?
<answer name="c4half">
<award><ceil>log(1/2)/log(0.96)</ceil></award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute="true" credit="0.8" feedbackText="Since we measure the lead concentration once a week, your answer should be an integer."><math>log(1/2)/log(0.96)</math></award>
</answer></p>

$c4half.feedbacks

<hint>
  <title>Need help?</title>

  <p>For the last question where we changed the elimination rate to 4%, you need to repeat the logic that you used for the 11% elimination rate. You need to determine the new formula for <m>c_{t+1}</m>, plug that into the function iteration applet, and see how long it takes for the concentration to drop to 1/2 of the original concentratoin.</p>
  
<hint>
  <title>Need more help?</title>

  <p>Here are the steps you can go through. If the concentation drops by 4% each week, what number do you need to multiply the concentration by each week?
    <answer weight="0" name="ans96">0.96</answer>
  </p>
    <feedback condition="$ans96.creditAchieved &lt; 1 and $ans96.numSubmissions > 3">
      You need to multiply by <m>1- 0.04 = 0.96</m>
    </feedback>

  <p>The new model is <m>c_{t+1} = </m>
    <answer weight="0" name="ans04ct">0.96 c_t</answer>.
  </p>
      <feedback condition="$ans04ct.creditAchieved &lt; 1 and $ans04ct.numSubmissions > 3">
      The new model is
        <me>c_{t+1} = 0.96c_t.</me>
    </feedback>

  <p>Enter the new model into the function iteration applet (but just use <m>c</m> for <m>c_t</m>). It doesn't matter what you use for the initial concentration <m>c_0</m>. Click the iterate button until you see when the concentration dropped to below half of the initial concentration.
  </p>

</hint>
  
</hint>
</section>

